## ⚙️ Configuration

### 🔹 Switch Configuration (SW1)

```cisco
vlan 10
 name HR
vlan 20
 name IT
vlan 30
 name SALES

interface range fa0/1 - 2
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 10

interface range fa0/3 - 4
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 20

interface range fa0/5 - 6
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 30

interface fa0/24
 switchport mode trunk
 switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20,30



🔹 Router Configuration (Router-on-a-Stick)

interface g0/0
 no shutdown

interface g0/0.10
 encapsulation dot1Q 10
 ip address 192.168.10.1 255.255.255.0

interface g0/0.20
 encapsulation dot1Q 20
 ip address 192.168.20.1 255.255.255.0

interface g0/0.30
 encapsulation dot1Q 30
 ip address 192.168.30.1 255.255.255.0



🧪 Verification & Testing
On Switch

show vlan brief
show interfaces trunk
show interfaces fa0/24 switchport

# On Router
show ip interface brief
show vlans

Inter-VLAN Ping Test:

ping 192.168.20.10     # From VLAN 10 PC to VLAN 20
ping 192.168.30.10     # From VLAN 10 PC to VLAN 30

🔍 Key Concepts Demonstrated

VLAN Segmentation
Access Ports vs Trunk Ports
802.1Q Encapsulation
Router-on-a-Stick (Subinterfaces)
Inter-VLAN Routing
Network Security through Segmentation


⚠️ Issues Encountered & Resolutions

Issue: No communication between VLANs
Solution: Added encapsulation dot1Q on subinterfaces
Issue: VLAN traffic not passing through trunk
Solution: Configured switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20,30
Issue: PCs in same VLAN cannot communicate
Solution: Verified access port VLAN assignment


📈 Conclusion
This project successfully implemented VLAN segmentation and Inter-VLAN Routing using Router-on-a-Stick method. It reflects real-world enterprise network practices for better security, performance, and manageability.


